@* Named for what it does rather than for what it returns: it scores a backlog,
   where "Recommendations" would promise a shortlist. *@
@page "/scoring"
@using System.Globalization
@using AniQueue.Core.Domain
@using AniQueue.Core.Jobs
@using AniQueue.Core.Recommendations
@using AniQueue.Core.Settings
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IRecommendationService Recommender
@* The sizes in effect, and where they are written. Two injections rather than one
   because reading and writing are genuinely different: the monitor reports what any
   configuration source currently says, while the store is the only thing that
   changes the file. *@
@inject IOptionsMonitor<ScoringOptions> ScoringOptions
@inject IUserSettingsStore Settings
@* The second courier. Injected even when nothing is configured, because
   whether an endpoint exists is a question the card asks it. *@
@inject IScoringEndpoint Endpoint
@* One model, one request at a time, and Test connection is the only thing here that
   still talks to it. Asked of the registry so the button can stand aside while the
   scoring task is running rather than queueing behind it. *@
@inject ITaskRegistry Tasks
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject ILogger<Scoring> Logger

<PageTitle>Scoring — AniQueue</PageTitle>

<div class="page-header">
    <h1>Scoring</h1>
    @* Says up front that this ranks and does not reorder. The queue shortening by
       itself already surprises people; a second thing writing to the backlog
       is easier to accept when its limits were stated before it ran, not after. *@
    <p>
        Hand your backlog to a model, and bring its ranking back. It scores what you
        are planning to watch — it never touches your Up Next order.
    </p>
</div>

<BusyDialog State="_busy" />

@* Confirmations only. A save that failed says so in a notice that stays until it is
   dealt with; a save that worked has nothing to leave behind but the fact that it
   happened, and silence is indistinguishable from a button that does nothing. *@
<Toast State="_toasts" />

@if (_error is not null)
{
    <div class="notice error" role="alert">@_error</div>
}

@if (_applied is not null)
{
    <div class="notice success" role="status">@_applied</div>
}

@* Two ways to get a ranking, and one surface for what comes back. The shape
   is the Sources page's, deliberately: a card per route, its settings behind a
   disclosure, and a preview that replaces the cards rather than joining them. Two
   pages answering one question in two idioms is worse than either idiom. *@
@if (_preview is null)
{
    @* Answers "is there anything left to do?" before either card offers to do it.
       Collapsed, because the answer is usually yes-or-no and the numbers behind it
       are only wanted when it is no — and coloured, because that answer should be
       readable without reading.

       Stale has a particular sense here: a score is not wrong because time passed, it
       is wrong because the history it was predicted against has grown. So this goes
       amber when there is work, and green only when every waiting title carries a
       ranking made against the taste the user has now. *@
    @if (_coverage is { Waiting: > 0 } coverage)
    {
        <div class="card status @(coverage.IsSettled ? "settled" : "attention")">
            <details>
                <summary>@CoverageHeadline(coverage)</summary>

                <dl class="scoring-facts">
                    <div>
                        <dt>Up to date</dt>
                        <dd>@coverage.UpToDate</dd>
                    </div>
                    @if (coverage.Stale > 0)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <dt>Stale</dt>
                            <dd>@coverage.Stale</dd>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @if (coverage.Unranked > 0)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <dt>Never ranked</dt>
                            <dd>@coverage.Unranked</dd>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @* A total, and only when there are parts to total. These are shares
                       of one backlog, so when the backlog is entirely one share the
                       total is that share printed a second time — "up to date 180,
                       waiting 180" said nothing twice and left the reader working out
                       whether the two numbers were meant to differ.

                       It is also no longer called "waiting", which meant "waiting to be
                       watched" here and "waiting to be ranked" two cards down: one word
                       for two counts on one page, one of which was wrong. *@
                    @if (coverage.Parts > 1)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <dt>Total</dt>
                            <dd>@coverage.Waiting</dd>
                        </div>
                    }
                </dl>

                @if (coverage.Stale > 0)
                {
                    <p class="muted">
                        A ranking goes stale once you have finished and rated
                        @_staleAfterRatings more @Plural(_staleAfterRatings, "title", "titles")
                        since it was made — what the model knew about your taste has moved on
                        since it guessed.
                    </p>
                }
            </details>
        </div>
    }

    <div class="card">
        <h2>Manual</h2>

        @if (_request is null)
        {
            <p class="muted">
                Build a file describing what you are planning to watch, paste it into any
                model you like, and bring the answer back. The same request the remote
                option sends, carried by you — and the route that works with any model,
                because you can see what came back before it is applied.
            </p>

            <div class="toolbar">
                <button class="primary" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning" @onclick="BuildAsync">
                    Build the request
                </button>
            </div>
        }
        else if (_request.Candidates.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="empty-state">
                <p>Nothing is waiting to be watched, so there is nothing to rank.</p>
                <a class="button primary" href="backlog">Open the backlog</a>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="muted">
                @_request.Candidates.Count @Plural(_request.Candidates.Count, "title", "titles") to rank,
                with @_request.History.Count of your @_request.HistoryAvailable scored
                @Plural(_request.HistoryAvailable, "title", "titles") as history.
                @* The size is the number that decides whether a model can read this at
                   all, and nothing else on the page implies it — a count of titles says
                   nothing about how much context they occupy. *@
                About @Size(_payload!.Length) of text.
            </p>

            @if (_capped is { } capped)
            {
                <p class="muted">
                    This request covers @capped of your @_waiting waiting
                    @Plural(_waiting, "title", "titles") — the ones longest without a score.
                    Build it again after applying to work through the rest.
                </p>
            }

            @if (_request.IsRankingLimited)
            {
                <p class="muted">
                    It asks for the best @_request.ExpectedResults back, not all
                    @_request.Candidates.Count. Every title is still weighed; only the top of
                    the result is returned.
                </p>
            }

            @if (_request.IsHistoryCapped)
            {
                <p class="muted">
                    The history is a sample, most recently finished first — enough for a model
                    to read your taste without crowding out the backlog it is ranking.
                </p>
            }

            @* One thing to carry, offered two ways. There were four buttons here — copy
               the request, copy the instructions, download the request, rebuild — which
               made the first decision on this card "which two of these do I need, and in
               what order do they go into the box?" when the answer was always both, in
               one order. The instructions are useless without the request and the request
               is unreadable without the instructions, so they travel together. *@
            <div class="toolbar">
                <button class="primary" @onclick="CopyDocumentAsync">Copy the whole prompt</button>
                <button @onclick="DownloadDocumentAsync">Download it as a file</button>
                <button @onclick="BuildAsync" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning">Regenerate</button>
            </div>

            @if (_copied is not null)
            {
                <p class="muted" role="status">@_copied</p>
            }

            <p class="muted">
                Paste it into your model as one message. It opens with what to do and ends
                with your backlog. If your model will not take a message this long, send the
                file instead — most will read an attachment they will not read as text.
            </p>

            @* Shown rather than only offered, because on a self-hosted install over plain
               http the clipboard API does not exist and the fallback can be refused too.
               Text on the page can always be selected by hand, so the copy button is a
               convenience over a route that already works. *@
            <details>
                <summary>Read the whole prompt</summary>
                <pre class="payload">@_document</pre>
            </details>
        }

        <div class="field">
            <label for="response">Paste the reply</label>
            @* The placeholder comes from a C# string rather than being written inline
               with entities. Razor encodes a literal attribute value, so a &quot; in the
               markup reaches the browser as &amp;quot; and the field advertises the
               wrong shape — quotation marks are exactly what a person is checking their
               reply against here. *@
            <textarea id="response" rows="8" spellcheck="false"
                      placeholder="@ResponseExample"
                      disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                      @bind="_response" @bind:event="oninput"></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="toolbar">
            <button class="primary" disabled="@(_busy.IsRunning || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_response))"
                    @onclick="PreviewPastedAsync">
                Check the reply
            </button>

            <InputFile id="response-file" class="file-input" OnChange="OnFileSelectedAsync"
                       accept=".json,.txt" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       aria-label="Upload the model's reply" />
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        @* Experimental, and the badge is the honest half of a two-part statement: the
           other half is that scheduled runs are off until somebody turns them on.
           Three models were tried against one real library and only one of them could
           answer — gpt-oss-20b managed it at 564 rated titles, while gemma-4-12b
           failed even at 50 and qwen3.5 returned nothing at 564. The two that failed
           did so by spending their whole output budget reasoning and never starting
           the JSON, which is a disposition rather than a bug and not one AniQueue can
           settle from this side. *@
        <h2>Remote model <span class="badge experimental">Experimental</span></h2>

        <p class="muted">
            Whether this works depends on the model. Some spend their whole reply
            thinking and never answer, and there is no setting here that fixes that —
            so scheduled ranking is off until you turn it on, and the
            <strong>Manual</strong> route above works with anything.
        </p>

        @if (!Endpoint.IsConfigured)
        {
            @* The normal state of a fresh install, so it is the state that has to read
               well. It says what to do rather than what is wrong. *@
            <p class="muted">
                No model is configured. Point AniQueue at one that speaks the
                chat-completions API — LM Studio, Ollama, llama.cpp — and it can rank
                your backlog without anything being copied by hand. No account and no
                API key: the model runs on your own hardware.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="muted">
                Ranking with <strong>@(Endpoint.Model ?? "the configured model")</strong>
                at <strong>@Endpoint.Endpoint</strong>. Ranking happens on the
                <a href="settings">Settings</a> page, with nobody waiting.
            </p>

            @if (_waitingCount > 0)
            {
                @* The size of the question, beside the size of the answer. Only the
                   output was ever bounded, which is why a request of twenty-five titles
                   could still be refused for being too long to read — the history
                   travels with every one of them and is by far the larger half. A
                   number nobody can see is one nobody can act on. *@
                <p class="muted">
                    A request sends @HistorySent scored
                    @Plural(HistorySent, "title", "titles") as history — roughly
                    @Tokens(EstimatedRequestTokens) to read. Your model needs a context
                    window at least that large.
                </p>
            }
        }

        @if (_testResult is { } test)
        {
            <div class="notice @(test.Succeeded ? "success" : "error")" role="status">
                <strong>@TestHeadline(test)</strong>

                @if (test.Diagnostic is { } diagnostic)
                {
                    @* Bounded, body only, and behind a disclosure. It is what
                       somebody debugging their own server needs and very little of
                       what anything else does. *@
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show what came back</summary>
                        <pre class="payload">@diagnostic</pre>
                    </details>
                }
            </div>
        }

        @* The soft guard went with the run it guarded. It warned that a request for
           four hundred rankings at once would not fit, and offered to send twenty-five
           instead — a choice that only existed because somebody was about to wait for
           the answer. The sweep asks in batches by construction and halves one that
           will not fit, so the hazard is handled where the request is made rather than
           in front of the person making it. *@

        <div class="toolbar">
            @* The one outbound call left outside the runner, so it is the one thing
               that can collide with a sweep. A self-hosted model answers one request at
               a time, and a test fired into a batch queues behind it and looks like a
               timeout. Asking the registry is smaller than a lock and more honest:
               the user learns the model is busy instead of watching a test hang. *@
            <button disabled="@(_busy.IsRunning || !Endpoint.IsConfigured || IsRanking)"
                    @onclick="TestAsync">
                Test connection
            </button>

            @if (IsRanking)
            {
                <span class="muted">Ranking is running — test when it has finished.</span>
            }
        </div>

        @* Settings behind a disclosure, like every per-source setting on Sources. The
           7b note that a disclosure "does not read as settings you can change" was
           learned about a panel floating above a request summary on a page with no
           card structure, and that page is what this replaces. *@
        <details class="source-settings" open="@(!Endpoint.IsConfigured)">
            <summary>Connection</summary>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="scoring-endpoint">Where your model is listening</label>
                <input id="scoring-endpoint" type="text" spellcheck="false" maxlength="200"
                       placeholder="http://192.168.1.50:1234" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_endpoint" @bind:event="oninput" />
                <p class="muted">
                    LM Studio serves <code>http://localhost:1234</code> and Ollama
                    <code>http://localhost:11434</code> by default. AniQueue adds the rest
                    of the address itself.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="scoring-model">Which model</label>
                <input id="scoring-model" type="text" spellcheck="false" maxlength="200"
                       placeholder="Whatever your server calls it" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_model" @bind:event="oninput" />
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="scoring-timeout">Give up after (seconds)</label>
                <input id="scoring-timeout" type="number" min="5" max="3600"
                       disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_timeoutSeconds" @bind:event="oninput" />
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="scoring-structured">
                    <input type="checkbox" id="scoring-structured" checked="@_structuredOutput"
                           @onchange="e => _structuredOutput = (bool)(e.Value ?? false)" />
                    Ask the server for JSON only
                </label>
                <p class="muted">
                    Leave this on unless your server rejects the request. It stops a model
                    wrapping its answer in explanation, which AniQueue can cope with but
                    would rather not have to.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="toolbar">
                <button class="primary" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning" @onclick="SaveConnectionAsync">
                    <Icon Name="save" />Save
                </button>
            </div>

            @* Its own disclosure rather than a field among the connection details.
               These are the settings that spend electricity while nobody is watching,
               and burying them under the same triangle as a hostname makes them easy
               to switch on without reading what they do. *@
            <details class="source-settings">
                <summary>Run on a schedule</summary>

                <p class="muted">
                    AniQueue can rank on its own — never touching your Up Next order, and
                    never applying a reply it could not read in full. It works through the
                    titles that have never been ranked first, then the ones your later
                    ratings have overtaken.
                </p>

                @* How often is set on the Settings page now, once for every background
                   task. What stays here is what a run may do when it happens. *@
                <p class="muted">
                    How often it looks is set on the <a href="settings">Settings</a> page, along
                    with every other kind of background work. A run stops as soon as there
                    is nothing out of date, so leaving it on costs nothing on a backlog
                    that is already ranked.
                </p>

                <div class="field">
                    <label for="scoring-batch">Titles per unattended request</label>
                    <input id="scoring-batch" type="number" min="5" max="500"
                           disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                           @bind="_batchSize" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    <p class="muted">
                        Smaller batches fit a smaller model. If one turns out to be too big to
                        read, AniQueue halves it and carries on rather than giving up.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="field">
                    <label for="scoring-budget">Stop after (minutes)</label>
                    <input id="scoring-budget" type="number" min="1" max="1440"
                           disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                           @bind="_sweepMinutes" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    <p class="muted">
                        How long one run may keep your model busy. Whatever it does not reach
                        is picked up by the next one.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="field">
                    <label for="scoring-stale">Re-rank after this many new ratings</label>
                    <input id="scoring-stale" type="number" min="0" max="1000"
                           disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                           @bind="_staleAfterRatings" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    <p class="muted">
                        A ranking is only as good as the taste it was predicted against, so it
                        goes out of date when you rate more shows rather than when time passes.
                        Nobody has to pick an interval — it follows how much you actually
                        watch. Zero leaves existing rankings alone for good.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="toolbar">
                    <button class="primary" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning" @onclick="SaveScheduleAsync">
                        <Icon Name="save" />Save
                    </button>
                </div>
            </details>

            <p class="muted">
                Saved to <code>@Settings.Path</code>, which is where to change it by hand if
                this page is ever unreachable.
            </p>
        </details>
    </div>

    @* Lifted out of both cards, because it governs a run down either route. Drawn in
       one card it would otherwise be drawn in two, which is two controls over one
       setting. Same lift, same reason, as the title language control on Sources. *@
    <div class="card">
        @* Bounded by the library rather than by an arbitrary ceiling. These offered
           anything up to five thousand — the clamp the storage happens to apply — on a
           library with a hundred and eighty titles waiting and five hundred scored, so
           every value past the real count meant the same thing as the real count while
           looking like a choice. A control whose range is mostly fiction teaches that
           its numbers do not matter, which is the opposite of what these need to
           teach: this is the page where a number too large is how a request stops
           fitting in a model. *@
        <details class="source-settings">
            <summary>Settings</summary>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="candidate-limit">Titles to rank</label>
                @* Lowering this lowers the other one with it. Without that the ceiling
                   moved and the value did not, leaving "at most 25" printed under a
                   field reading 180 — a control contradicting its own caption. *@
                <input id="candidate-limit" type="number" min="1" max="@_waitingCount"
                       placeholder="All @_waitingCount of them" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_candidateLimit" @bind:event="oninput"
                       @bind:after="ClampReturnTop" />
                <p class="muted">
                    Leave this empty to offer everything you are planning to watch — all
                    @_waitingCount of them, and it stays "everything" as your backlog grows.
                    If your model struggles with a long list, set a number: each request then
                    takes the titles that have gone longest without a score, so running it
                    again works through the rest.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="return-top">Rankings to ask for back</label>
                @* "All N of them", the same sentence the two fields either side of it use
                   when empty. It read "One for every title", which is the same fact
                   said a different way — and three controls that all mean everything when
                   left alone should not need three readings to establish that. The number
                   is what a limit here is measured against, so saying it is also what makes
                   a typed value obviously smaller or obviously pointless. *@
                <input id="return-top" type="number" min="1" max="@MaxReturnTop"
                       placeholder="All @MaxReturnTop of them" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_returnTop" @bind:event="oninput" />
                <p class="muted">
                    Leave this empty to get a ranking for every title you send — all
                    @MaxReturnTop of them, because nothing can come back that was not offered.
                    Set a number and the model still weighs every title; it just returns the
                    best of them. Worth setting when the reply is the slow part: a ranking
                    with a sentence of reasoning per title is generated a word at a time, and
                    a long one can run out of room halfway down the list.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label for="history-size">Scored titles to send as history</label>
                <input id="history-size" type="number" min="1" max="@_scoredCount"
                       placeholder="All @_scoredCount of them" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_historySize" @bind:event="oninput" />
                <p class="muted">
                    Most recently finished first, out of the @_scoredCount titles you have
                    scored. More history means a ranking that fits your taste more closely, up
                    to the point where it crowds out the backlog in your model's context — it
                    is the larger half of a request, and the usual reason one is too big to
                    read. Leave it empty to send all @_scoredCount, which is the biggest a
                    request gets.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="toolbar">
                <button class="primary" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning" @onclick="SaveSizesAsync">
                    <Icon Name="save" />Save
                </button>
            </div>
        </details>
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* One card while a preview is up, and only the card that produced it. Sources
       learned this the hard way: rendering every card let each draw its own Discard,
       so two sources meant two ways out of one preview. A preview is one route's
       answer, so it is one route's card.

       <b>A heading and nothing else</b>, which is where this departs from Sources.
       There the collapsed card carries the Discard because the preview below it has
       none; here the preview pairs Discard with Apply at the point the decision is
       actually made, so a second one up here would be the same two-ways-out bug in a
       new shape. What is left is the one thing the preview cannot say for itself:
       which route produced it. *@
    <div class="card collapsed">
        <h2>Manual</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h3>What this would do</h3>

        @* One notice, carrying its own severity, rather than a heading followed by a
           stack of separate banners. The colour is the summary: green means the
           reply needed nothing said about it, and that reassurance was previously
           expressed by showing nothing at all — which is indistinguishable from a
           page that has not finished thinking.

           Problems are listed inside it rather than beside it, because they are the
           reasons for the colour rather than separate news. *@
        <div class="notice @Status" role="@(_preview.HasErrors ? "alert" : "status")">
            <strong>@Headline()</strong>

            @if (_preview.Problems.Count > 0)
            {
                <ul class="notice-list">
                    @* Errors first whatever order they arrived in: with one present
                       nothing applies at all, so it is the only line that changes what
                       happens next. *@
                    @foreach (var problem in _preview.Problems.OrderByDescending(p => p.Severity))
                    {
                        <li>@problem.Message</li>
                    }
                </ul>
            }
        </div>

        @{
            @* Always asked now. This was skipped for a remote run, which knew what had
               answered because the server said so in its own reply; there is no such
               run any more, so anything previewed here came out of somebody else's
               chat window and only they can say which model it was.

               <b>Deliberately left empty, and deliberately not asked of the model.</b>
               Both shortcuts fill this in by themselves and both fill it in wrongly.
               Asking the model gets a fine-tune claiming to be ChatGPT and a quantised
               build that does not know its own quant; carrying the previous run's
               answer forward gets last week's ChatGPT stamped on today's local model.
               This is the field somebody consults when deciding whether a better model
               is worth re-running for, so a wrong answer costs more than no answer —
               and neither shortcut is distinguishable, afterwards, from something a
               person typed. *@
            <div class="field model-field">
                <label for="model-identifier">Which model produced this?</label>
                <input id="model-identifier" type="text" maxlength="100"
                       placeholder="@ModelPlaceholder" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning"
                       @bind="_modelIdentifier" @bind:event="oninput" />
                <p class="muted">
                    Recorded with the ranking and shown beside every score it writes, so a
                    score from a weaker model can be told apart later. Leave it blank if you
                    are not sure — an unspecified model is worth more than a wrong one.
                </p>
            </div>
        }

        @* Above the table, not below it. A ranking runs to a couple of hundred rows,
           and a decision that has already been described in the notice above should
           not be at the far end of them: it is the first thing wanted after reading
           what would happen, and the last thing reachable.

           "Apply 182 scores" says the whole ranking, not this page of it, which is
           the one thing paging could otherwise make ambiguous. *@
        <div class="toolbar">
            <button class="primary" disabled="@(_busy.IsRunning || !_preview.CanApply)"
                    @onclick="ApplyAsync">
                Apply @_preview.ApplicableCount @Plural(_preview.ApplicableCount, "score", "scores")
            </button>
            <button @onclick="Discard" disabled="@_busy.IsRunning">Discard</button>
        </div>
        @if (_preview.Items.Count > 0)
        {
            @* The numeric headers carry the class their cells do, so a right-aligned
               column does not sit under a left-aligned label. *@
            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table class="ranking">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            @* No Rank column. The rows arrive ordered by
                               predicted score, which is the order the backlog will
                               show them in, so the position is readable off the table
                               without a number claiming to be the model's own. *@
                            <th scope="col">Title</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="numeric">Score</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="numeric">Confidence</th>
                            <th scope="col">Why</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var item in VisibleItems)
                        {
                            <tr class="@(item.WillApply ? null : "muted")">
                                @* The flex wrapper goes inside the cell, never on it.
                                   A td set to display:flex stops being a table cell —
                                   it leaves the column sizing entirely, so a fixed
                                   layout cannot give it a width and it collapses to
                                   its content while the header above it stays put.
                                   Same structure as the backlog's rows. *@
                                <td>
                                    <div class="title-cell"><span>@item.Title</span></div>
                                    @if (item.SkippedBecause is { } reason)
                                    {
                                        <div class="row-meta">Skipped — @reason</div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                @* "was" only when it changed. Applying the same ranking
                                   twice, or re-checking a reply already applied, otherwise
                                   prints "10, was 10" down the whole table — a second line
                                   on every row that says nothing, and the noise arrives
                                   exactly when the least is happening. *@
                                <td class="numeric">
                                    @Score(item.Result.PredictedScore)
                                    @if (Changed(item) is { } previous)
                                    {
                                        <div class="row-meta">was @previous</div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                <td class="numeric">@Percent(item.Result.Confidence)</td>
                                <td>@item.Result.Reason</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            @* Paged in memory, unlike the backlog's, which pages in the database
               because it is reading several thousand rows it does not have. This is
               reading a list it already holds in full — the whole ranking arrived in
               one reply and has to be applied as one — so paging is purely a matter
               of how much to draw at once. Nothing is fetched by pressing these. *@
            @if (_preview.Items.Count > PreviewPageSize)
            {
                <div class="toolbar">
                    <button @onclick="() => _previewSkip = 0" disabled="@(_previewSkip == 0)">First</button>
                    <button @onclick="() => _previewSkip -= PreviewPageSize"
                            disabled="@(_previewSkip == 0)">Previous</button>
                    <span class="muted">
                        @(_previewSkip + 1)–@Math.Min(_previewSkip + PreviewPageSize, _preview.Items.Count)
                        of @_preview.Items.Count
                    </span>
                    <button @onclick="() => _previewSkip += PreviewPageSize"
                            disabled="@(_previewSkip + PreviewPageSize >= _preview.Items.Count)">Next</button>
                    <button @onclick="() => _previewSkip = (_preview.Items.Count - 1) / PreviewPageSize * PreviewPageSize"
                            disabled="@(_previewSkip + PreviewPageSize >= _preview.Items.Count)">Last</button>
                </div>
            }
        }
    </div>
}

@if (_runs.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="card">
        <h2>Past rankings</h2>

        @* Ten, down from twenty. A scheduled sweep writes one run per batch rather than
           one per sweep, so an evening against a real backlog fills this table with its
           own batches and pushes out everything a person came here to compare against.
           Ten is what fits on screen beside the card above it. *@
        <p class="muted">The last @RunsShown, newest first.</p>

        @* Kept because a ranking is the one thing here written by something that is
           not the user, and "when did this score arrive, and from what" is the
           question asked when one looks wrong. *@
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">When</th>
                        @* "From" was one column collapsing the route and the model, so a run
                           that named its model stopped saying how it arrived. They are
                           separate questions: the route explains what a run is, the model
                           is what makes two runs worth comparing. *@
                        <th scope="col">Type</th>
                        <th scope="col">Model</th>
                        @* Right-aligned to match their cells. Left as plain headers they
                           sat over the far edge of the numbers they label, which on a
                           two-column pair of counts reads as a column boundary in the
                           wrong place. *@
                        <th scope="col" class="numeric">Ranked</th>
                        <th scope="col" class="numeric">History</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var run in _runs)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@run.CreatedAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("g", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)</td>
                            <td>@run.ProviderName</td>
                            <td>
                                @if (run.ModelIdentifier is { } model)
                                {
                                    @model
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span class="muted">Unspecified</span>
                                }
                            </td>
                            <td class="numeric">@run.ResultCount of @run.CandidateCount</td>
                            <td class="numeric">@run.CompletedCount</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which route produced the preview on screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Held by the page rather than carried on <c>ScoringPreview</c>, which stays
    /// ignorant of how its reply arrived — that ignorance is what makes the two routes
    /// one contract. The page needs it anyway to render only the card that
    /// produced the preview, so asking the model name of a manual run costs nothing
    /// extra.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <summary>What a run records about how it was carried.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Free text on <c>RecommendationRun</c>, shown to users in its own column.
    /// The vocabulary is Manual and Scheduled: anything this page produces arrived by
    /// hand. Old runs keep the value they were written with, so <c>Past rankings</c>
    /// can still show a route that no longer exists.
    /// </remarks>
    private const string ProviderName = "Manual";

    /// <summary>
    /// Above this, asking for every ranking in one reply is a bad bet.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <summary>Whether the scoring task is running right now.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Asked of the registry rather than held here, because what is running is the
    /// runner's business and this page is one of several that could be open. It
    /// replaces IScoringGate, which arbitrated between a sweep and a run started from
    /// this page — and there is no such run any more.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool IsRanking =>
        Tasks.Snapshot().Any(task => task.TaskKey == "scoring" && task.IsRunning);

    private ScoringEndpointResult? _testResult;

    private string? _endpoint;
    private string? _model;
    private int _timeoutSeconds;
    private bool _structuredOutput;


    /// <summary>Titles in the visible backlog, which is what a run covers.</summary>
    private int _waitingCount;

    /// <summary>Scored titles that exist, which is the most history there can be.</summary>
    private int _scoredCount;

    private ScoringCoverage? _coverage;

    private int _staleAfterRatings = 5;


    private int _batchSize = 25;
    private int _sweepMinutes = 60;

    private async Task SaveScheduleAsync()
    {
        _batchSize = Math.Clamp(_batchSize, 5, 500);
        _sweepMinutes = Math.Clamp(_sweepMinutes, 1, 1440);
        _staleAfterRatings = Math.Clamp(_staleAfterRatings, 0, 1000);

        var saved = await Settings.SaveAsync(Settings.Read() with
        {

            ScoringBatchSize = _batchSize,
            ScoringSweepMinutes = _sweepMinutes,
            ScoringStaleAfterRatings = _staleAfterRatings
        });

        _error = saved.Saved ? null : $"Could not save to {saved.Path}: {saved.Error}";

        if (saved.Saved)
        {
            await _toasts.ShowAsync("Schedule saved");
        }

        // The staleness threshold decides what the card above counts, so the card has
        // to be recounted rather than left describing the rule that was in force a
        // moment ago.
        await CountWaitingAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The one line the card is read for.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Three states rather than two, because "nothing has been ranked yet" and "some
    /// of this is out of date" call for different things: the first is an invitation
    /// to start, the second a nudge to run it again. Collapsing them into "not up to
    /// date" would tell a new user their empty backlog has gone off.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string CoverageHeadline(ScoringCoverage coverage) => coverage switch
    {
        { IsUntouched: true } => "No titles have been ranked yet",
        { IsSettled: true } => "All rankings up to date",
        { Stale: > 0, Unranked: > 0 } => $"{coverage.Unranked} unranked, {coverage.Stale} out of date",
        { Stale: > 0 } => $"{coverage.Stale} {Plural(coverage.Stale, "ranking is", "rankings are")} out of date",
        _ => $"{coverage.Unranked} {Plural(coverage.Unranked, "title has", "titles have")} never been ranked"
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The most rankings that could come back, which is however many were offered.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Follows the candidate limit rather than the backlog: asking for fifty back out
    /// of a request that offers twenty-five is not a smaller answer, it is an
    /// impossible one, and the preview would then report twenty-five omissions the
    /// user never chose.
    /// </remarks>
    private int MaxReturnTop => Math.Max(Math.Min(_candidateLimit ?? _waitingCount, _waitingCount), 1);

    /// <summary>
    /// Brings the return limit down when the offer it depends on shrinks.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Only ever downwards. Raising the candidate limit again does not restore a
    /// number the user has not asked for a second time — a setting that moves on its
    /// own in both directions is one nobody can predict, and the one direction that
    /// has to move is the one that would otherwise leave an impossible value on
    /// screen.
    /// </remarks>
    private void ClampReturnTop()
    {
        if (_returnTop is { } top && top > MaxReturnTop)
        {
            _returnTop = MaxReturnTop;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>What a request would cost, measured once per load.</summary>
    private ScoringSizeEstimate? _estimate;

    /// <summary>
    /// Roughly how much a run would ask the model to read.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Measured from two real requests at startup rather than estimated from a formula,
    /// because a candidate's size depends on how many identifiers and title variants it
    /// carries and that varies by library. Four characters to the token is the usual
    /// rule of thumb, and this is a rule-of-thumb number: it exists to tell somebody
    /// whether they are near their model's limit, not to predict the tokeniser.
    /// </remarks>
    private int EstimatedRequestTokens
    {
        get
        {
            if (_estimate is not { } estimate)
            {
                return 0;
            }

            var candidates = Math.Min(_candidateLimit ?? _waitingCount, _waitingCount);

            return estimate.CharactersFor(candidates) / 4;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A token count as a person would read it.</summary>
    private static string Tokens(int tokens) => tokens < 1000
        ? $"{tokens} tokens"
        : $"{tokens / 1000.0:0.#}K tokens";

    /// <summary>
    /// The shape the field expects, shown while it is empty.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Abbreviated to the wrapper the parser actually requires. A full example would
    /// be too wide for the field and would wrap into something that no longer looks
    /// like the JSON it is describing.
    /// </remarks>
    private const string ResponseExample = """{ "results": [ … ] }""";

    private BusyScope _busy = default!;
    private ToastScope _toasts = default!;
    private IJSObjectReference? _module;

    private ScoringRequest? _request;
    /// <summary>The request as JSON, which is what the size on the card measures.</summary>
    private string? _payload;

    /// <summary>Instructions and request as the single thing a person carries.</summary>
    private string? _document;

    private string? _response;
    private ScoringPreview? _preview;

    /// <summary>How many past runs the table shows. See the note beside it.</summary>
    private const int RunsShown = 10;

    private IReadOnlyList<RecommendationRunSummary> _runs = [];

    private int? _historySize = ScoringRequestOptions.Default.MaxHistory;

    /// <summary>How many scored titles a request would actually carry.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The setting says how many at most; this says how many there are. They differ only
    /// when the field is empty, which now means all of them rather than none of them.
    /// </remarks>
    private int HistorySent => Math.Min(_historySize ?? _scoredCount, _scoredCount);
    private int? _candidateLimit;
    private int? _returnTop;

    /// <summary>What the user says produced the ranking. Recorded with the run.</summary>
    private string? _modelIdentifier;

    /// <summary>
    /// How many rows of a ranking to draw at once.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Half the backlog's fifty, because these rows are taller: each carries a
    /// sentence of reasoning, and a screen of them is the point of reading the
    /// preview at all.
    /// </remarks>
    private const int PreviewPageSize = 25;

    private int _previewSkip;

    /// <summary>The rows currently drawn. Applying is never limited to these.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<ScoringPreviewItem> VisibleItems =>
        _preview!.Items.Skip(_previewSkip).Take(PreviewPageSize);

    /// <summary>How many titles are waiting, when the request covers only some.</summary>
    private int _waiting;

    private string? _error;
    private string? _copied;
    private string? _applied;

    /// <summary>How many the request covers, or null when it covers all of them.</summary>
    private int? _capped => _request is { } request && request.IsCandidatesCapped
        ? request.Candidates.Count
        : null;


    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _busy = new BusyScope(() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged));
        _toasts = new ToastScope(() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged));
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Read from the settings file rather than from the service, which no longer
        // owns a setting. The monitor gives the value currently in effect,
        // including one an environment variable supplied, so the inputs show what a
        // request would actually use rather than only what this page last wrote.
        var current = ScoringOptions.CurrentValue;
        var options = current.ToRequestOptions();

        _historySize = options.MaxHistory;
        _candidateLimit = options.MaxCandidates;
        _returnTop = options.ReturnTop;

        _endpoint = current.Endpoint;
        _model = current.Model;
        _timeoutSeconds = current.TimeoutSeconds;
        _structuredOutput = current.UseStructuredOutput;


        _batchSize = current.BatchSize;
        _sweepMinutes = current.SweepMinutes;

        await LoadRunsAsync();
        await CountWaitingAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// How many titles are waiting, without building a request to find out.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The Remote card says what a run would cover before anybody asks for one, and
    /// building a whole request to render a number would mean loading the backlog on
    /// every page view. A request that is capped still reports the full count on it,
    /// so this is the cheapest thing that answers the question honestly.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task CountWaitingAsync()
    {
        // One call, and it is the service that decides how to answer it. This was
        // two BuildRequestAsync calls, which read and serialised every rated title twice
        // to render one number — four times a visit, because this page prerenders and
        // initialises again when its circuit connects.
        //
        // The notes flag travels with it, which the old pair did not carry: a request
        // built with personal notes is bigger than one without, so leaving it out
        // under-reported the size for exactly the person who had opted in.
        _estimate = await Recommender.MeasureAsync(
            Profile.DefaultProfileId,
            ScoringRequestOptions.From(
                _historySize,
                candidateLimit: null,
                includePersonalNotes: ScoringOptions.CurrentValue.IncludePersonalNotes));

        _waitingCount = _estimate.CandidatesAvailable;
        _scoredCount = _estimate.HistoryAvailable;

        _staleAfterRatings = ScoringOptions.CurrentValue.StaleAfterRatings;
        _coverage = await Recommender.GetCoverageAsync(Profile.DefaultProfileId, _staleAfterRatings);
    }

    private async Task BuildAsync()
    {
        Reset();

        var options = ScoringRequestOptions.From(
            _historySize,
            _candidateLimit,
            _returnTop,
            ScoringOptions.CurrentValue.IncludePersonalNotes);

        _request = await _busy.RunAsync(
            "Building the request",
            _ => Recommender.BuildRequestAsync(Profile.DefaultProfileId, options));

        _waiting = _request.CandidatesAvailable;

        // Rendered once and held, so the file, the clipboard and the text on screen
        // are three views of one string rather than three builds of it.
        _payload = ScoringRequestWriter.Write(_request);

        // Instructions first, then the request. A model reads the whole message either
        // way, but the person pasting it reads the top — and what they need to see is
        // that this is a set of instructions, not a wall of JSON that begins nowhere.
        _document = ScoringPromptBuilder.Build(_request)
            + Environment.NewLine
            + Environment.NewLine
            + _payload;
    }

    // A ranking arrives from the sweep with nobody present, or by hand through the
    // paste route below. Nothing on this page opens an outbound scoring request that
    // somebody is waiting on.

    private async Task TestAsync()
    {
        Reset();

        _testResult = await _busy.RunAsync(
            "Testing the connection",
            _ => Endpoint.TestAsync());

        // A server that answers and cannot produce a ranking is a distinct outcome
        // from one that does not answer, and it is the whole reason this sends a real
        // request rather than a ping. The parser decides which happened.
        if (_testResult.Succeeded && new ScoringResponseParser().Parse(_testResult.Reply!).HasErrors)
        {
            _testResult = _testResult with
            {
                Failure = ScoringEndpointFailure.Unreadable,
                Message = "It answered, but not with a ranking AniQueue could read. "
                    + "Try turning on \"Ask the server for JSON only\", or a larger model.",
                Diagnostic = _testResult.Reply
            };
        }
    }

    private string TestHeadline(ScoringEndpointResult test) => test.Succeeded
        ? $"Answered in {Elapsed(test.Duration)}"
            + (test.ModelIdentifier is { } model ? $" using {model}." : ".")
        : test.Message ?? "It did not answer.";

    private async Task SaveConnectionAsync()
    {
        var saved = await Settings.SaveAsync(Settings.Read() with
        {
            ScoringEndpoint = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_endpoint) ? null : _endpoint.Trim(),
            ScoringModel = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_model) ? null : _model.Trim(),
            ScoringTimeoutSeconds = Math.Clamp(_timeoutSeconds, 5, 3600),
            ScoringUseStructuredOutput = _structuredOutput
        });

        _error = saved.Saved ? null : $"Could not save to {saved.Path}: {saved.Error}";

        // Cleared because it describes a connection that may no longer be the one
        // configured: a green "answered in 1s" sitting under a hostname somebody has
        // just changed is a claim about the wrong server.
        _testResult = null;

        if (saved.Saved)
        {
            await _toasts.ShowAsync("Connection saved");
        }
    }

    private async Task SaveSizesAsync()
    {
        // Bounded by the library before the storage bounds them by its own ceiling. The
        // max attributes above stop a spinner going past the real count, and a typed or
        // pasted number walks straight past those — a control that gates only in the
        // browser gates only the people who were not going to overshoot anyway.
        //
        // Asking for more than exists was never an error and never will be: the request
        // simply carries what there is. What it was, was a number on screen that did not
        // describe anything, on the one page where a number too large is how a request
        // stops fitting in a model.
        if (_historySize is { } history)
        {
            _historySize = Math.Clamp(history, 1, Math.Max(_scoredCount, 1));
        }

        if (_candidateLimit is { } candidates)
        {
            _candidateLimit = Math.Clamp(candidates, 1, Math.Max(_waitingCount, 1));
        }

        if (_returnTop is { } top)
        {
            _returnTop = Math.Clamp(top, 1, MaxReturnTop);
        }

        var options = ScoringRequestOptions.From(_historySize, _candidateLimit, _returnTop);

        var saved = await Settings.SaveAsync(Settings.Read() with
        {
            ScoringHistorySize = options.MaxHistory,
            ScoringCandidateLimit = options.MaxCandidates,
            ScoringReturnTop = options.ReturnTop
        });

        // Clamped values back into the inputs, so a number the settings refused does
        // not sit on screen looking like it was accepted.
        _historySize = options.MaxHistory;
        _candidateLimit = options.MaxCandidates;
        _returnTop = options.ReturnTop;

        _error = saved.Saved ? null : $"Could not save to {saved.Path}: {saved.Error}";

        if (saved.Saved)
        {
            await _toasts.ShowAsync("Sizes saved");
        }

        // Re-measured, because the size on the Remote card is derived from the history
        // that was current when it was taken. Without this the card answers "40 scored
        // titles — roughly 27.1K tokens", which is the old cost beside the new count and
        // is worse than showing nothing: the number exists to be acted on, and one that
        // does not move when the setting does teaches that acting on it is pointless.
        await CountWaitingAsync();
    }

    private async Task DownloadDocumentAsync()
    {
        // Plain text rather than .json, because it is no longer only JSON: a file named.
        // json that opens with a paragraph of English is a file that lies about itself,
        // and some upload boxes decide what they will accept from the extension.
        var name = $"aniqueue-prompt-{DateTime.Now:yyyy-MM-dd}.txt";

        if (!await InvokeModuleAsync<bool>("downloadText", name, _document!, "text/plain"))
        {
            _error = "The file could not be saved. The prompt is below and can be copied by hand.";
        }
    }

    private Task CopyDocumentAsync() => CopyAsync(_document!, "Prompt");

    private async Task CopyAsync(string text, string what)
    {
        _copied = null;
        _error = null;

        // The interop returns whether it reached the clipboard, because on plain
        // http neither route is available and silence would look like success.
        var copied = await InvokeModuleAsync<bool>("copyText", text);

        if (copied)
        {
            _copied = $"{what} copied.";
        }
        else
        {
            _error = $"{what} could not be copied — your browser blocks it outside a "
                + "secure connection. Open the panel below and copy it by hand.";
        }
    }

    private async Task PreviewPastedAsync() => await PreviewAsync(_response!, _request);

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads a reply from a file, for a model whose output was saved rather than
    /// pasted.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Read into the textarea rather than previewed straight from the file, so the
    /// two routes converge before anything is checked: what is previewed is always
    /// what is on screen, and a reply that needs one character fixed can be fixed
    /// where it is rather than re-saved and re-uploaded.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task OnFileSelectedAsync(InputFileChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        Discard();

        try
        {
            _response = await _busy.RunAsync($"Reading {e.File.Name}", async _ =>
            {
                // The parser enforces the same ceiling again on what it reads, so the
                // limit holds whichever way in was used.
                await using var stream = e.File.OpenReadStream(ScoringLimits.Default.MaxBytes);
                using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);

                return await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
            });

            await PreviewAsync(_response, _request);
        }
        catch (IOException ex)
        {
            Logger.LogWarning(ex, "Recommendation upload rejected");
            _error = "That file is too large to read.";
        }
    }

    private async Task PreviewAsync(string json, ScoringRequest? request = null)
    {
        _error = null;
        _applied = null;

        _preview = await _busy.RunAsync(
            "Checking the reply",
            // Pasted, for both ways in: the upload route reads the file into the textarea
            // and comes through here, so what is checked is always a document a person
            // carried, which is the route a reply has to name its database on.
            _ => Recommender.PreviewAsync(Profile.DefaultProfileId, ScoringRoute.Pasted, json, request));

        // A new ranking starts at its own beginning. Left alone, checking a second
        // reply while on page four opens it four pages in, or on nothing at all when
        // the new one is shorter.
        _previewSkip = 0;
    }

    private async Task ApplyAsync()
    {
        // Whatever the user typed, or nothing. A run carried by hand has no server to
        // ask what answered it — that was the remote route's advantage and it went
        // with the route.
        var model = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_modelIdentifier) ? null : _modelIdentifier.Trim();

        var result = await _busy.RunAsync(
            "Applying the ranking",
            progress => Recommender.ApplyAsync(
                Profile.DefaultProfileId,
                _preview!,
                ProviderName,
                model,
                progress,

                // Null, always: nothing here measures how long somebody else's chat
                // window took. The sweep still records its own duration.
                duration: null));

        _applied = result.Skipped == 0
            ? $"Scored {result.Applied} {Plural(result.Applied, "title", "titles")}."
            : $"Scored {result.Applied} {Plural(result.Applied, "title", "titles")}, "
                + $"and skipped {result.Skipped}.";

        Discard();
        await LoadRunsAsync();
        await CountWaitingAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Clears the reply and its preview, leaving the request alone.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Deliberately asymmetric. Discarding a ranking is a decision about that reply;
    /// throwing away the request as well would mean rebuilding it to try a second
    /// model, which is exactly what somebody discarding a bad reply is about to do.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Discard()
    {
        _preview = null;
        _response = null;
        _error = null;
        _previewSkip = 0;
    }

    private void Reset()
    {
        Discard();
        _applied = null;
        _copied = null;
    }

    private async Task LoadRunsAsync() =>
        _runs = await Recommender.GetRunsAsync(Profile.DefaultProfileId, RunsShown);

    // The model name is never carried forward from the previous run. A pre-filled
    // value looks exactly like a typed one, so a wrong answer in this field costs
    // more than no answer.

    /// <summary>
    /// An example of what to type, rather than a note that typing is optional.
    /// </summary>
    private const string ModelPlaceholder = "e.g. GPT-5, or qwen2.5-14b";

    /// <summary>
    /// Calls the module, and treats an unavailable browser as a failure rather than
    /// as an error.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing here is load-bearing: every route it offers has the payload on the
    /// page behind it. So a JS failure reports and the page keeps working, in the
    /// same spirit as Up Next's drag handle — letting the exception escape would
    /// tear down the circuit and replace a missing convenience with an error page.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task<T?> InvokeModuleAsync<T>(string name, params object?[] args)
    {
        try
        {
            _module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>(
                "import", "./Components/Pages/Scoring.razor.js");

            return await _module.InvokeAsync<T>(name, args);
        }
        catch (JSException ex)
        {
            Logger.LogWarning(ex, "Clipboard or download interop failed");
            return default;
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            return default;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Which of the three notice colours the preview earns.</summary>
    private string Status => _preview switch
    {
        { HasErrors: true } => "error",
        { Problems.Count: > 0 } => "warning",
        _ => "success"
    };

    /// <summary>The one sentence the colour is summarising.</summary>
    private string Headline()
    {
        if (_preview!.HasErrors)
        {
            // States the consequence rather than the count, because with an error
            // present the count is not what happens next — nothing is.
            return "Nothing will be applied. A ranking that is wrong in part cannot be "
                + "told apart from a whole one afterwards, so fix the reply or ask again.";
        }

        var scores = $"{_preview.ApplicableCount} {Plural(_preview.ApplicableCount, "score", "scores")}";

        return _preview.Problems.Count > 0
            ? $"{scores} will be applied. Worth reading first:"
            : $"{scores} will be applied, and nothing about the reply needs your attention.";
    }

    private static string Score(double value) =>
        value.ToString("0.#", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// The score this row replaces, or null when it replaces nothing or replaces it
    /// with the same number.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Compared as displayed rather than as stored, so 8.64 and 8.6 do not count as a
    /// change the table cannot show. A difference nobody can see is not one worth a
    /// line of its own.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string? Changed(ScoringPreviewItem item)
    {
        if (item.PreviousScore is not { } previous)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var was = Score(previous);

        return was == Score(item.Result.PredictedScore) ? null : was;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Roughly how much text a request is, in the units a context window is discussed
    /// in.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Characters rather than tokens, and said as "about". Tokens are what a model
    /// actually counts, but the ratio depends on its tokeniser and on how much of the
    /// payload is Japanese — so a token figure would be a precise number that is
    /// wrong, where an approximate size is honest and still tells someone whether
    /// they are near their limit.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Size(int characters) => characters < 2048
        ? $"{characters} characters"
        : $"{(characters / 1024.0).ToString("0.#", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)} KB";

    private static string Percent(double value) =>
        ((int)Math.Round(value * 100)).ToString(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture) + "%";

    private static string Plural(int count, string one, string many) => count == 1 ? one : many;

    /// <summary>
    /// A duration as somebody waiting on it would say it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Seconds while the number is small enough to read as progress, then minutes and
    /// seconds. "0:47" is a clock rather than a wait, and "203 seconds" is arithmetic.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Elapsed(TimeSpan elapsed) => elapsed.TotalMinutes < 1
        ? $"{(int)elapsed.TotalSeconds}s"
        : $"{(int)elapsed.TotalMinutes}m {elapsed.Seconds:00}s";

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // Cancels a dismissal still counting down, so a toast shown just before the
        // user navigated does not wake up to notify a page that has gone.
        _toasts.Dispose();

        if (_module is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                await _module.DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
                // The circuit ended before the module could be released.
            }
        }
    }
}
